BOARD-SPECIFIC GPIO OWNERSHIP

Raspberry Pi Pico: on-board LED is directly connected to GP25.
Raspberry Pi Pico W: LED is on CYW43 WL_GPIO0, NOT RP2040 GP25.
Pico W reserves RP2040 GP23/24/25 for wireless-chip functions;
GP29 also serves an internal wireless-chip function.
PicoNIX blocks generic GPIO access to these reserved pins on Pico W.
Use pins, ls /dev/gpio, and the documented device interfaces.
Do not attempt to reconfigure or probe reserved pins via GPIO/PWM.
A successful echo to a GPIO node only proves that the write was
accepted; it does not prove the LED, buzzer or attached circuit moved.
Request an external observation when physical verification matters.
On this teaching setup GP18 has a buzzer and GP19 has a servo.
They share PWM slice 1 frequency: see /man/pwm-workflow.
